{
  "version": "2.1.0",
  "$schema": "https://json.schemastore.org/sarif-2.1.0.json",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-307",
              "name": "Brute-force rate limit on POST /api/connect.php bypassed by cookie rotation (per-session counter, no IP/global limiter)",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Brute-force rate limit on POST /api/connect.php bypassed by cookie rotation (per-session counter, no IP/global limiter)"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The brute-force protection on the Namecheap API-key connect endpoint is enforced only per browser session. Failed connect attempts are tracked in the PHP session (`$_SESSION['nc_attempts']`) with a 5-attempts/10-minute lockout, but there is no IP-based or global limiter: an attacker who simply does not send the session cookie gets a fresh zero counter on every request and can guess Namecheap API keys through the server indefinitely."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The brute-force protection on the Namecheap API-key connect endpoint is enforced only per browser session. Failed connect attempts are tracked in the PHP session (`$_SESSION['nc_attempts']`) with a 5-attempts/10-minute lockout, but there is no IP-based or global limiter: an attacker who simply does not send the session cookie gets a fresh zero counter on every request and can guess Namecheap API keys through the server indefinitely.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker can use the publicly reachable landing server as an unthrottled proxy for Namecheap API-key guessing: every request without a session cookie returns 401 and resets nothing, so the 5-attempts/10-minute lockout never applies. In production (real `api.namecheap.com`), this enables sustained key-guessing campaigns through the server's IP, abuse of the server as a relay to evade Namecheap-side throttling, and service degradation of the landing app itself. The per-session counter provides no real protection against any automated client.\n\nEnforce the failed-attempt invariant outside the session: add an IP-based (or global) failed-attempt counter in `php/bootstrap.php` alongside the existing session counter, so that exceeding the threshold returns 429 regardless of cookie state. The narrowest repository-native change is to extend `register_failed_attempt()` to also record attempts per remote address in a file store under `CRED_DIR` (already a 0700 credential directory with an atomic tmp+rename write precedent in `store_credentials()`), checked before the session counter; `clear_failed_attempts()` should then remove the per-IP entry on successful connect so legitimate users who mistyped a key are not stranded. Complement (not replace) this with nginx `limit_req` on `location /api/` for defense in depth, and consider a small per-request delay or upstream-response cache for repeated identical guesses to blunt relay-abuse. Fix embedded in this report as a one-click suggestion block covering `register_failed_attempt()` and `clear_failed_attempts()`.",
                "markdown": "The brute-force protection on the Namecheap API-key connect endpoint is enforced only per browser session. Failed connect attempts are tracked in the PHP session (`$_SESSION['nc_attempts']`) with a 5-attempts/10-minute lockout, but there is no IP-based or global limiter: an attacker who simply does not send the session cookie gets a fresh zero counter on every request and can guess Namecheap API keys through the server indefinitely.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker can use the publicly reachable landing server as an unthrottled proxy for Namecheap API-key guessing: every request without a session cookie returns 401 and resets nothing, so the 5-attempts/10-minute lockout never applies. In production (real `api.namecheap.com`), this enables sustained key-guessing campaigns through the server's IP, abuse of the server as a relay to evade Namecheap-side throttling, and service degradation of the landing app itself. The per-session counter provides no real protection against any automated client.\n\nEnforce the failed-attempt invariant outside the session: add an IP-based (or global) failed-attempt counter in `php/bootstrap.php` alongside the existing session counter, so that exceeding the threshold returns 429 regardless of cookie state. The narrowest repository-native change is to extend `register_failed_attempt()` to also record attempts per remote address in a file store under `CRED_DIR` (already a 0700 credential directory with an atomic tmp+rename write precedent in `store_credentials()`), checked before the session counter; `clear_failed_attempts()` should then remove the per-IP entry on successful connect so legitimate users who mistyped a key are not stranded. Complement (not replace) this with nginx `limit_req` on `location /api/` for defense in depth, and consider a small per-request delay or upstream-response cache for repeated identical guesses to blunt relay-abuse. Fix embedded in this report as a one-click suggestion block covering `register_failed_attempt()` and `clear_failed_attempts()`."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-307",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/307.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0002",
              "name": "Session fixation on Namecheap connect — client-chosen session ID accepted and never rotated on authenticated transition",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Session fixation on Namecheap connect — client-chosen session ID accepted and never rotated on authenticated transition"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The landing app session uses PHP's default non-strict session mode and never rotates the session identifier when a Namecheap API key is connected, so an attacker can pre-set the session cookie to a known value and inherit the authenticated connection established on it."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The landing app session uses PHP's default non-strict session mode and never rotates the session identifier when a Namecheap API key is connected, so an attacker can pre-set the session cookie to a known value and inherit the authenticated connection established on it.\n\nAny anonymous visitor can be locked into an attacker-known session ID. When the Namecheap connect flow completes on that session, the attacker replays the known cookie value and inherits the connected state, gaining full unauthorized access to the victim's Namecheap account data and domain/DNS configuration through the landing app's connected session.\n\nSet session.use_strict_mode=1 via ini_set before session_start in bootstrap.php so client-supplied session IDs are rejected, and call session_regenerate_id(true) on the nc_connected transition in php/connect.php (and equivalently on any other privilege change) so the anonymous pre-auth session ID is invalidated and a fresh server-issued ID is installed when a connection is established.",
                "markdown": "The landing app session uses PHP's default non-strict session mode and never rotates the session identifier when a Namecheap API key is connected, so an attacker can pre-set the session cookie to a known value and inherit the authenticated connection established on it.\n\nAny anonymous visitor can be locked into an attacker-known session ID. When the Namecheap connect flow completes on that session, the attacker replays the known cookie value and inherits the connected state, gaining full unauthorized access to the victim's Namecheap account data and domain/DNS configuration through the landing app's connected session.\n\nSet session.use_strict_mode=1 via ini_set before session_start in bootstrap.php so client-supplied session IDs are rejected, and call session_regenerate_id(true) on the nc_connected transition in php/connect.php (and equivalently on any other privilege change) so the anonymous pre-auth session ID is invalidated and a fresh server-issued ID is installed when a connection is established."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.6",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0003",
              "name": "Global credentials file shared by all sessions — last-connector-wins cross-user overwrite",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Global credentials file shared by all sessions — last-connector-wins cross-user overwrite"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "All connected sessions share one global credentials file, so each new Namecheap connection silently overwrites every other session's stored key and every connected session acts on whoever connected last."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "All connected sessions share one global credentials file, so each new Namecheap connection silently overwrites every other session's stored key and every connected session acts on whoever connected last.\n\nAny second connection silently replaces the first session's stored Namecheap key. The first session then reports the wrong account owner and proxies its domain/DNS calls through the second user's key, giving unauthorized access to another user's Namecheap account data while the first user's credentials are silently discarded.\n\nScope the credential store to the session that created it: derive the credentials path from the PHP session ID (for example CRED_DIR . '/' . hash('sha256', session_id()) . '.namecheap.json') in store_credentials(), read_credentials(), and delete_credentials(), and keep the existing lock-plus-atomic-rename write pattern. Alternatively, encrypt the stored credentials and bind the encryption key to the session so only the creating session can decrypt them; both approaches remove the last-connector-wins overwrite.",
                "markdown": "All connected sessions share one global credentials file, so each new Namecheap connection silently overwrites every other session's stored key and every connected session acts on whoever connected last.\n\nAny second connection silently replaces the first session's stored Namecheap key. The first session then reports the wrong account owner and proxies its domain/DNS calls through the second user's key, giving unauthorized access to another user's Namecheap account data while the first user's credentials are silently discarded.\n\nScope the credential store to the session that created it: derive the credentials path from the PHP session ID (for example CRED_DIR . '/' . hash('sha256', session_id()) . '.namecheap.json') in store_credentials(), read_credentials(), and delete_credentials(), and keep the existing lock-plus-atomic-rename write pattern. Alternatively, encrypt the stored credentials and bind the encryption key to the session so only the creating session can decrypt them; both approaches remove the last-connector-wins overwrite."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.9",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "vuln-0004",
              "name": "Unauthenticated status.php leaks CSRF token, mailcow origin, public IP, and last connector's username",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Unauthenticated status.php leaks CSRF token, mailcow origin, public IP, and last connector's username"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "An unauthenticated GET /api/status.php leaks the session CSRF token, mailcow origin, server public egress IP, and the last connector's Namecheap username to any anonymous visitor."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "warning"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "An unauthenticated GET /api/status.php leaks the session CSRF token, mailcow origin, server public egress IP, and the last connector's Namecheap username to any anonymous visitor.\n\nAny anonymous visitor learns the server's public egress IP (the value a Namecheap operator needs to whitelist), the mailcow origin, and — when a connection exists — the last connector's Namecheap username and sandbox flag, materially aiding the credential-abuse and account-proxying attack chains against this deployment.\n\nGate the connection-state fields behind require_namecheap_session() so connected, username, and sandbox are only reported to connected sessions, and remove detect_public_ip() and the session CSRF token from the unauthenticated response (the CSRF token should only be minted for sessions that actually need it for a state-changing request). Keep Cache-Control: no-store so the gated response is never cached.",
                "markdown": "An unauthenticated GET /api/status.php leaks the session CSRF token, mailcow origin, server public egress IP, and the last connector's Namecheap username to any anonymous visitor.\n\nAny anonymous visitor learns the server's public egress IP (the value a Namecheap operator needs to whitelist), the mailcow origin, and — when a connection exists — the last connector's Namecheap username and sandbox flag, materially aiding the credential-abuse and account-proxying attack chains against this deployment.\n\nGate the connection-state fields behind require_namecheap_session() so connected, username, and sandbox are only reported to connected sessions, and remove detect_public_ip() and the session CSRF token from the unauthenticated response (the CSRF token should only be minted for sessions that actually need it for a state-changing request). Keep Cache-Control: no-store so the gated response is never cached."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "5.3",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              }
            },
